<style>
  /* ============================================================
     HERO — editorial split. Copy on the left, a light "spec
     index card" on the right that teases the essentials and
     drops the eye down into the offer.
     ============================================================ */
  .svc-hero {
    position: relative;
    /* Was 72/63 — asymmetric by 9px, which is not enough to read as
       intentional and is exactly enough to look like a mistake. Now one step
       plus a shoulder above (it sits under the nav) and one clean step below. */
    padding-block: calc(var(--band-md) + var(--band-sm) * 0.4) var(--band-md);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-hairline);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .svc-hero-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4.5rem);
    align-items: center;
  }
  @media (min-width: 940px) {
    .svc-hero-grid { grid-template-columns: 1.25fr 0.9fr; }
  }
  .svc-hero-copy { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: 1.5rem; }
  /* "get online" is italic and carries a g. .display runs line-height 0.98,
     which clips it at this size. Overridden here rather than in global.css,
     which the locked homepage shares. */
  /* step-5, not step-6: at step-6 this headline runs to three lines at
     desktop in a 1.25fr column. Two is the cap. */
  .svc-hero-title {
    font-size: var(--text-step-5); margin: 0; max-width: 16ch;
    line-height: 1.1; padding-bottom: 0.06em;
  }
  .svc-hero-lede { max-width: 46ch; margin: 0; }
  .svc-hero-actions { display: flex; gap: 1rem; flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: 0.5rem; }

  /* ============================================================
     HERO VISUAL — eight page thumbnails, built from the same
     wireframe primitives the hero showcase uses on the homepage.
     ============================================================ */
  .svc-pages-fig { margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.1rem; }
  .svc-pages { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: clamp(0.5rem, 1vw, 0.85rem); }
  /* One grid cell, two stacked layers. Grid rather than absolute positioning
     so both layers inherit the card's padding automatically — the padding
     shrinks at mobile and hard-coded insets would drift out of sync. */
  .pg {
    /* Held as a variable because the designed layer bleeds back out over it
       (see .pg-art) and the two have to stay in step when mobile shrinks it. */
    --pg-pad: 8px;
    aspect-ratio: 3 / 4;
    display: grid;
    grid-template: 1fr / 1fr;
    padding: var(--pg-pad);
    border-radius: 8px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-line);
    background: var(--color-paper);
    box-shadow: 0 14px 30px -20px rgb(7 23 34 / 0.30);
    /* Thumbnails this small have no slack: anything a design layer overshoots
       by gets clipped at the rounded frame instead of spilling into the grid. */
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .pg-wire, .pg-art {
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
    min-width: 0; min-height: 0;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column;
  }
  .pg-wire { gap: 5px; }
  .pg-nav { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; margin-bottom: 1px; }
  .pg-logo { width: 14px; height: 4px; border-radius: 2px; background: rgb(13 47 69 / 0.16); }
  .pg-links { display: flex; gap: 3px; margin-left: auto; }
  .pg-links span { width: 7px; height: 3px; border-radius: 1px; background: rgb(13 47 69 / 0.12); }
  .pg-bar { width: 84%; height: 7px; border-radius: 2px; background: rgb(13 47 69 / 0.18); }
  .pg-bar-2 { width: 58%; }
  .pg-line { width: 70%; height: 3px; border-radius: 1px; background: rgb(13 47 69 / 0.12); }
  .pg-media {
    flex: 1; min-height: 0; margin-top: 1px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    border: 1px dashed rgb(13 47 69 / 0.20);
    /* Was a diagonal hatch. Flat fill instead — the dashed border is already
       the unambiguous "no image here yet" signal, and the hatch was the last
       gradient function left in this figure. */
    background: rgb(13 47 69 / 0.06);
  }
  .pg-pill { width: 26px; height: 8px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--color-periwinkle); opacity: 0.55; margin-top: auto; }
  /* Slight variation so eight thumbnails read as eight pages, not a tiled texture. */
  .pg:nth-child(3n) .pg-bar { width: 66%; }
  .pg:nth-child(3n) .pg-bar-2 { width: 44%; }
  .pg:nth-child(4n) .pg-line { width: 52%; }

  /* ---------- The designed layer ----------------------------------------
     Same primitive vocabulary as the wireframe — bars, blocks, pills — but
     painted the way a finished website is painted rather than the way a
     blueprint is.

     Why this is drawn in solid colour: the previous version of this layer
     used translucent navy (0.30-0.55 alpha) for every mark, and the blank
     wireframe underneath uses the SAME hue at 0.12-0.20. Adding shapes in
     the same wash the placeholder is made of produces a more detailed
     wireframe, not a design — the thing still looked like it was loading.
     So every mark below is an opaque colour.

     EIGHT PALETTES, ONE STANDARD. Each page below declares its own complete
     palette and none of them inherit from each other — eight different client
     sites, which is the point of the graphic. The previous set of eight was
     rebuilt because seven of them were badly CONSTRUCTED, not because they
     were different from one another. Variety was never the defect.

     The one page that worked was 02 · SERVICES, and every other palette here
     was built to pass the four tests it passes. Read this before adding or
     editing a page:

       1. THREE VALUE TIERS, NOTHING MUDDY BETWEEN THEM. A light ground around
          L*90-97, a deep ink around L*8-25, and rules/body copy in between.
          Nothing that defines the page may sit in the L*40-60 band. That band
          is what made the old About portrait (a #8c6a55 taupe at L*47 with no
          dark anywhere on the page) and the old Gallery mosaic (two tiles at
          L*54 and L*58, touching) read as unfinished. Clustered mid-lightness
          was the actual defect in all seven, not the hues.
       2. TWO HUES MAXIMUM, ROUGHLY 60-30-10. Ground 60, ink and image mass 30,
          accent 10. Several pages here are one hue plus neutrals, which is
          also fine; what is not fine is a third hue turning up.
       3. ONE ACCENT, TWO MARKS MAXIMUM. The old home page put its amber on
          five separate marks, which stops being an accent and becomes a second
          brand colour with nothing left to draw the eye.
       4. ONE FOCAL SHAPE THAT SURVIVES A SQUINT, AND CONTENT THAT FILLS THE
          FRAME. Blur the card: exactly one thing should still be obviously
          heaviest, and there should be no dead space around a floating
          cluster.

     Layouts differ as hard as the palettes do, and the two are chosen
     together — each page has its dominant dark mass in a different PLACE:
     a hero (01), a pushed row (02), a left column (03), the whole page (04),
     a lead tile (05), a centre column (06), a repeated stack (07), a cover
     band (08).

     Where the quality comes from, now that nothing is drawn: proportion and
     spacing rhythm first (one two-step scale, below, used by all eight pages),
     then type hierarchy carried in bar HEIGHT and length — 8px hero headline,
     7px page headline, 4px subhead, 2px body, with real rag — then alignment,
     then the single accent doing all the chromatic work. Nothing in these
     eight cells is placed by eye. If a cell looks thin the answer is to fix
     its proportions, never to draw something in it.

     On contrast: the figure is aria-hidden and decorative, but a sighted
     low-vision reader still has to make it out. The faintest mark in the set
     is body copy, which runs ~2.9:1 or better against its own page colour;
     headlines, buttons and photographs run 5:1 and up. The old floor was
     1.9:1, so going opaque improved this rather than risking it. */
  .pg-art {
    /* Two steps, and only two. Every gap in every one of the eight designs is
       one of these, so the whole set shares one spacing rhythm — which is most
       of what separates a comp that reads as designed from one that reads as
       assembled. --a-gap separates page sections and sets body leading;
       --a-gap-tight is for marks that belong to the same component. Both step
       down together at mobile so the rhythm survives the shrink. */
    --a-gap: 5px;
    --a-gap-tight: 3px;
    gap: var(--a-gap);
    /* Bleed back out over the card's padding. A real page has no white
       margin around it, and the paper colour is half of what makes each of
       the eight read as a different site — it has to reach the card edge.
       The padding is re-added inside so the content insets still match the
       wireframe layer at both breakpoints. */
    margin: calc(var(--pg-pad) * -1);
    padding: var(--pg-pad);
    background: var(--a-paper);
    /* opacity:0 is the resting state — the wireframe is what you see until
       the block scrolls in. The reduced-motion block at the end of this
       section overrides it. */
    opacity: 0;

    /* Neutral fallbacks only. Every one of the eight .pg-* blocks below
       declares its OWN complete palette, so nothing here should ever actually
       paint a page — these exist so a mark is never unstyled if a page grows
       an element its palette forgot. Do not put a real colour here and let
       pages inherit it: that is how eight pages end up looking like one. */
    --a-paper: #ffffff;
    --a-header: #ffffff;
    --a-header-line: #e4e7ec;
    --a-nav-ink: #16233a;
    --a-nav-link: #8d97a6;
    --a-ink: #16233a;
    --a-ink-mid: #4d596b;
    --a-ink-soft: #98a2b0;
    --a-line: #e0e4ea;
    --a-fill: #f2f4f8;
    --a-accent: #144667;
    /* Image blocks: one flat tone. That is the entire control surface. */
    --a-img-bg: #3f4d75;
  }

  /* ===== Shared primitives — unchanged from services.astro ===== */
  .a-nav {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 4px;
    flex: none; overflow: hidden;
    height: 13px;
    margin: calc(var(--pg-pad) * -1) calc(var(--pg-pad) * -1) 0;
    padding-inline: var(--pg-pad);
    background: var(--a-header);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--a-header-line);
  }
  .a-logo {
    position: relative;
    width: 10px; height: 3px; flex: none; border-radius: 1px;
    margin-left: 7px;
    background: var(--a-nav-ink);
  }
  .a-logo::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute; right: 100%; top: 50%;
    margin-right: 2px; transform: translateY(-50%);
    width: 5px; height: 5px; border-radius: 1px;
    background: var(--a-accent);
  }
  .a-links { display: flex; gap: 3px; margin-left: auto; }
  .a-links > span { width: 6px; height: 2px; border-radius: 1px; background: var(--a-nav-link); }
  .a-navcta { width: 13px; height: 7px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--a-accent); flex: none; }

  .a-h { height: 7px; border-radius: 2px; background: var(--a-ink); flex: none; }
  .a-h-sm { height: 4px; border-radius: 1px; background: var(--a-ink-mid); flex: none; }
  .a-l { height: 2px; border-radius: 1px; background: var(--a-ink-soft); flex: none; }
  .a-cta { width: 30px; height: 9px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--a-accent); flex: none; }
  .a-img, .pg-home .a-hero { border-radius: 3px; background: var(--a-img-bg); }

  /* ===== NEW: the footer band =========================================
     The single biggest lift available to this figure. Every card was
     nav → content → nothing, and a page with a header and no footer reads as
     a screenshot that got cropped rather than as a whole page. Built as the
     mirror of .a-nav: same bleed out over the card's padding, same header
     colour, so the two pages that carry a RULE instead of a filled masthead
     (03 and 05) automatically get a ruled footer too, and the six with a
     filled band get a filled one. Nothing per-page needs to opt in.

     margin-top: auto is a guard, not the layout — every page below fills its
     own height. It only matters at the narrowest viewports, where it keeps the
     footer welded to the bottom edge instead of floating up mid-card. */
  .a-foot {
    display: flex; align-items: center;
    flex: none;
    height: 10px;
    margin: auto calc(var(--pg-pad) * -1) calc(var(--pg-pad) * -1);
    padding-inline: var(--pg-pad);
    background: var(--a-header);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--a-header-line);
  }
  .a-foot-mark { width: 9px; height: 2px; border-radius: 1px; background: var(--a-nav-link); flex: none; }
  .a-foot-links { display: flex; gap: 3px; margin-left: auto; }
  .a-foot-links > span { width: 5px; height: 2px; border-radius: 1px; background: var(--a-nav-link); }

  /* ===== NEW: paragraph grouping ======================================
     A paragraph sets its lines at the TIGHT step and paragraphs separate at
     the wide one. That single distinction is what stops a column of 2px bars
     reading as a barcode — 03 and 08 both used one even 5px pitch for every
     line on the card, so there was no way to see where one thought ended. */
  .a-p { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); }

  /* ============================================================
     01 · HOME — GRAPHITE & COBALT. Palette unchanged.
     Bands: hero (~44%) → three feature cards → proof strip → footer.
     ============================================================ */
  .pg-home {
    --a-paper: #edeff3;
    --a-header: #0d1117;
    --a-header-line: #0d1117;
    --a-nav-ink: #ffffff;
    --a-nav-link: #8f97a5;
    --a-ink: #0d1117;
    --a-ink-mid: #464e5b;
    --a-ink-soft: #949ba8;
    --a-line: #d5d9e0;
    --a-fill: #ffffff;
    --a-accent: #2f6bff;
    --a-img-bg: #16294d;
  }
  /* Flex proportions rather than fixed heights, so the three bands keep their
     ratio at every card width instead of the hero eating whatever is left. */
  .pg-home .a-hero {
    flex: 5; min-height: 0;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--a-gap-tight);
    padding: 8px; border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .pg-home .a-hero .a-h { height: 8px; background: #ffffff; }
  .pg-home .a-hero .a-l { background: #93a4c4; margin-bottom: 2px; }
  .pg-home .a-cards { flex: 2; min-height: 0; display: flex; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); }
  .pg-home .a-card {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--a-gap-tight);
    padding: 5px; border-radius: 2px;
    background: var(--a-fill); border: 1px solid var(--a-line);
  }
  /* Ink, not accent. The accent is already spent on the nav CTA and the hero
     CTA; a third and fourth appearance turns it into a second brand colour. */
  .pg-home .a-card-mark { width: 6px; height: 6px; border-radius: 2px; background: var(--a-ink-mid); flex: none; }
  /* The proof strip — four logo marks under a hairline. Borderless on purpose:
     a second bordered box directly under the feature cards would read as one
     six-cell grid rather than as two bands. */
  .pg-home .a-strip {
    flex: 1.3; min-height: 0;
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--a-gap);
    padding-top: var(--a-gap-tight);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--a-line);
  }
  /* Not --a-line. At 4px on this paper the rule colour was invisible and the
     strip read as an empty band under the cards, which is the fault it was
     added to fix. This is the quietest tone that still registers as marks. */
  .pg-home .a-strip > span { flex: 1; height: 4px; border-radius: 1px; background: #bfc6d2; }

  /* ============================================================
     02 · SERVICES — FOREST & GOLD. Reference page, unchanged.
     ============================================================ */
  .pg-svc {
    --a-paper: #f7f5ef;
    --a-header: #174034;
    --a-header-line: #174034;
    --a-nav-ink: #ffffff;
    --a-nav-link: #9dbdb0;
    --a-ink: #174034;
    --a-ink-mid: #446458;
    --a-ink-soft: #8a9c92;
    --a-line: #d8d3c5;
    --a-fill: #ffffff;
    --a-accent: #d8a231;
  }
  .pg-svc .a-list { flex: 1; min-height: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); }
  .pg-svc .a-item {
    flex: 1; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--a-gap);
    padding-inline: var(--a-gap); border-radius: 3px;
    border: 1px solid var(--a-line); background: var(--a-fill);
  }
  .pg-svc .a-sq { width: 8px; height: 8px; border-radius: 2px; background: var(--a-accent); flex: none; }
  .pg-svc .a-item-copy {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap-tight);
  }
  .pg-svc .a-item.is-on { background: #174034; border-color: #174034; }
  .pg-svc .a-item.is-on .a-h-sm { background: #ffffff; }
  .pg-svc .a-item.is-on .a-l { background: #b6cdc2; }

  /* ============================================================
     03 · ABOUT — OXBLOOD & IVORY. Palette unchanged.
     ============================================================ */
  .pg-about {
    --a-paper: #f4efe6;
    --a-header: #f4efe6;
    --a-header-line: #2a1c18;
    --a-nav-ink: #2a1c18;
    --a-nav-link: #948877;
    --a-ink: #2a1c18;
    --a-ink-mid: #5c4c40;
    --a-ink-soft: #a3968a;
    --a-line: #ded4c4;
    --a-fill: #ffffff;
    --a-accent: #9e2b39;
    --a-img-bg: #5e1d24;
  }
  /* The portrait used to run the whole height of the card, which made it a
     tall flat slab of one colour — the single biggest thing wrong with this
     page. It is now one band of three: the split is ~36% of the card, a stats
     row sits under it, and a closing section takes the rest. The image is
     roughly square instead of a column, which is what stops it reading as a
     placeholder waiting to be filled. */
  .pg-about .a-split { flex: 3; min-height: 0; display: flex; gap: var(--a-gap); }
  .pg-about .a-portrait { width: 44%; flex: none; }
  .pg-about .a-copy {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0; overflow: hidden;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap-tight);
  }
  /* Three numbers under a rule — the standard about-page furniture, and the
     one band on this card that is neither image nor prose, which is what keeps
     the two apart. The number is set at headline weight; a stat whose figure
     is the same weight as its label is not a stat. */
  .pg-about .a-stats {
    flex: none; display: flex; gap: var(--a-gap);
    padding-top: var(--a-gap-tight);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--a-line);
  }
  .pg-about .a-stat { flex: 1; min-width: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); }
  .pg-about .a-close {
    flex: 2; min-height: 0; overflow: hidden;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap-tight);
  }

  /* ============================================================
     04 · CONTACT — INK & LIME. Replaces petrol & apricot outright. This is
     the one page in the set whose palette changed rather than only its
     layout.

     It stays the one INVERTED page, because that is 04's job in the group:
     eight cards need one value extreme built downward from white instead of
     upward from paper, and this is the card that can carry it — a contact
     form is mostly empty boxes, which is exactly what a dark ground makes
     look deliberate rather than unfinished.

     Tiers — masthead L*4 / paper L*10 / field fill L*17 / borders L*30 /
     body L*55 / labels L*84 / headline L*100, with the lime at L*90. Hues:
     one, and it is only ever the accent — everything structural is a cool
     neutral, so the submit is the single most saturated thing on the card by
     a wide margin. On a contact page the button is the thing you should be
     looking at.

     Two notes for anyone editing this. The paper is lifted off the masthead
     rather than the masthead pushed under the paper: at L*4 there is nowhere
     darker to go, so on a dark page the header seam has to come from the
     lighter side of it. And the field fill has to stay clearly ABOVE the
     paper — the fault that made the live card read as an empty dark
     rectangle was paper, fill and borders all sitting inside a 15-point
     band, and that is a trap this scheme can fall back into very easily. */
  .pg-contact {
    --a-paper: #16191d;
    --a-header: #08090b;
    --a-header-line: #454c55;
    --a-nav-ink: #ffffff;
    --a-nav-link: #878e98;
    --a-ink: #ffffff;
    --a-ink-mid: #ced3d9;
    --a-ink-soft: #7d848e;
    --a-line: #454c55;
    --a-fill: #262b32;
    --a-accent: #c9f24e;
  }
  .pg-contact .a-form { flex: 1; min-height: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap); }
  /* Label over field. A form without labels is a stack of boxes; at this size
     one 2px bar above each field is the entire difference. */
  .pg-contact .a-fieldset { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); flex: none; }
  .pg-contact .a-fieldset-tall { flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  .pg-contact .a-lab { height: 2px; border-radius: 1px; background: var(--a-ink-soft); flex: none; }
  .pg-contact .a-row { display: flex; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); flex: none; }
  .pg-contact .a-row > .a-fieldset { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }
  .pg-contact .a-field {
    height: 10px; flex: none; border-radius: 3px;
    border: 1px solid var(--a-line); background: var(--a-fill);
  }
  .pg-contact .a-field-tall { flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
  /* A real actions row: the fine print on the left, the button on the right,
     which is where a submit lives. The live version was a 58%-wide pill hard
     against the left edge with nothing opposite it. */
  .pg-contact .a-actions {
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--a-gap); flex: none;
  }
  .pg-contact .a-submit { height: 11px; width: 38%; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--a-accent); flex: none; }

  /* ============================================================
     05 · GALLERY — CHARCOAL & SIGNAL RED. Palette and mosaic unchanged.
     ============================================================ */
  .pg-gallery {
    --a-paper: #f1efec;
    --a-header: #f1efec;
    --a-header-line: #1c1a17;
    --a-nav-ink: #1c1a17;
    --a-nav-link: #9a9186;
    --a-ink: #1c1a17;
    --a-ink-mid: #57514a;
    --a-ink-soft: #a29a8f;
    --a-line: #ddd8d0;
    --a-fill: #ffffff;
    --a-accent: #c02f1d;
    --a-img-bg: #1c1a17;
  }
  /* Filter pills. Neutral, not accent — the accent is already on the logo mark
     and the nav CTA, and this page's whole scheme is "one red mark, nothing
     else saturated". The selected pill is filled in the page ink. */
  .pg-gallery .a-filters { display: flex; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); flex: none; }
  .pg-gallery .a-filters > span {
    width: 15px; height: 5px; border-radius: 999px;
    border: 1px solid var(--a-line); background: var(--a-fill);
  }
  .pg-gallery .a-filters > .is-on { background: var(--a-ink); border-color: var(--a-ink); }
  /* Nine landscape thumbnails on a 3-up grid, not five tall tiles on a 2-up.
     The old lead tile plus 2x2 gave every cell a near-square block of flat
     colour big enough to read as "image missing" rather than as a photograph
     in a grid. Nine cells at 4:3 are unmistakably a gallery: the eye reads the
     REPETITION, and no single block is large enough to look unfilled.
     Explicit aspect-ratio rather than 1fr rows, so the crop stays landscape
     however much height the band is given. */
  .pg-gallery .a-mosaic {
    flex: none;
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: var(--a-gap-tight);
  }
  .pg-gallery .a-mosaic .a-img { aspect-ratio: 4 / 3; }
  /* The same five-step warm-charcoal ladder as before, laid out so no two
     tiles sharing an edge are within 20 L* of each other — every seam in the
     grid is a real edge, and no row or column repeats a tone. */
  .pg-gallery .a-img:nth-child(1) { --a-img-bg: #1c1a17; }
  .pg-gallery .a-img:nth-child(2) { --a-img-bg: #a09789; }
  .pg-gallery .a-img:nth-child(3) { --a-img-bg: #332f2a; }
  .pg-gallery .a-img:nth-child(4) { --a-img-bg: #cfc9c0; }
  .pg-gallery .a-img:nth-child(5) { --a-img-bg: #332f2a; }
  .pg-gallery .a-img:nth-child(6) { --a-img-bg: #a09789; }
  .pg-gallery .a-img:nth-child(7) { --a-img-bg: #6b6259; }
  .pg-gallery .a-img:nth-child(8) { --a-img-bg: #cfc9c0; }
  .pg-gallery .a-img:nth-child(9) { --a-img-bg: #1c1a17; }
  /* Load more. Outline, centred, sitting on its own under the grid — the thing
     that says the nine tiles are the top of a set rather than all of it. */
  .pg-gallery .a-more {
    flex: none; align-self: center;
    width: 34%; height: 8px; border-radius: 999px;
    border: 1px solid var(--a-ink-soft);
    margin-top: auto;
  }

  /* ============================================================
     06 · PRICING — SLATE & JADE. Palette unchanged.
     ============================================================ */
  .pg-pricing {
    --a-paper: #eef0f1;
    --a-header: #1f2a30;
    --a-header-line: #1f2a30;
    --a-nav-ink: #ffffff;
    --a-nav-link: #8fa0a9;
    --a-ink: #1f2a30;
    --a-ink-mid: #52616a;
    --a-ink-soft: #97a3aa;
    --a-line: #d6dbde;
    --a-fill: #ffffff;
    --a-accent: #2e8b6f;
    --a-img-bg: #1f2a30;
  }
  /* flex: none, not flex: 1. Stretched to fill the card the columns became
     three tall boxes with a handful of marks adrift in them — the emptiest
     thing in the set. Sized by their own contents they come out at ~40% of the
     card, and the FAQ band below takes the height they were wasting. */
  .pg-pricing .a-plans { flex: none; display: flex; align-items: stretch; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); }
  /* Top-aligned, not centred. A pricing column is read downward — name, price,
     rule, features, button — and centring the whole stack floated it in the
     middle of an empty box, which is why this was the emptiest card in the set. */
  .pg-pricing .a-plan {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;
    gap: var(--a-gap-tight);
    padding: 4px; border-radius: 3px;
    border: 1px solid var(--a-line); background: var(--a-fill);
    /* This inset, not the picked column, is what sets the height of the whole
       row — the row is as tall as a side column plus both its margins. It came
       down from 7px because at 7 the row ran to 48% of the card and left the
       FAQ under it crushed into 15%. The picked column still stands visibly
       proud; it just does it inside a shorter band. */
    margin-block: 4px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .pg-pricing .a-num { width: 68%; height: 7px; border-radius: 2px; background: var(--a-ink); flex: none; }
  /* The rule under the price is what separates the header of the column from
     its list. Without it the price is just the largest bar in one long stack. */
  .pg-pricing .a-rule { width: 100%; height: 1px; background: var(--a-line); flex: none; margin-block: 1px; }
  .pg-pricing .a-feats {
    width: 100%;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;
    gap: var(--a-gap);
  }
  .pg-pricing .a-feats > .a-l { width: 82%; }
  .pg-pricing .a-plan > .a-l { width: 52%; }
  /* Pinned to the column's own bottom edge, so every column has a foot and the
     slack sits between the list and the button where a pricing card puts it.

     An outline, not a fill: the two side columns are secondary and the one
     filled button on the card belongs to the picked column. Filled in --a-line
     they were invisible against the white column and the columns still looked
     footless. */
  .pg-pricing .a-plan-cta {
    width: 76%; height: 7px; border-radius: 999px; flex: none;
    margin-top: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--a-ink-soft);
  }
  .pg-pricing .is-pick { margin-block: 0; background: var(--a-ink); border-color: var(--a-ink); }
  .pg-pricing .is-pick .a-num { background: var(--a-accent); }
  .pg-pricing .is-pick .a-l { background: #9fb3bd; }
  .pg-pricing .is-pick .a-rule { background: #46545c; }
  /* The one filled CTA on the card, in the page's own paper — on a slate
     column that is the widest value gap available, and it keeps the jade on
     the price where it does the most work. */
  .pg-pricing .is-pick .a-plan-cta { background: var(--a-fill); border-color: var(--a-fill); }

  /* The FAQ accordion. Every pricing page on the web ends in one, and it is
     the right filler here for two reasons: it is pure type, so it does not
     compete with the three columns above it for the eye, and its four ruled
     rows give the lower half of the card a rhythm the columns do not have. */
  .pg-pricing .a-faq {
    flex: 1; min-height: 0; overflow: hidden;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap-tight);
  }
  .pg-pricing .a-qs { flex: 1; min-height: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .pg-pricing .a-q {
    flex: 1;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--a-gap);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--a-line);
  }
  .pg-pricing .a-q:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .pg-pricing .a-plus { width: 4px; height: 4px; border-radius: 1px; background: var(--a-ink-soft); flex: none; }

  /* ============================================================
     07 · BLOG INDEX — AUBERGINE & ROSE. Palette unchanged.
     ============================================================ */
  .pg-blog {
    --a-paper: #f6f2f5;
    --a-header: #33223a;
    --a-header-line: #33223a;
    --a-nav-ink: #ffffff;
    --a-nav-link: #a693ad;
    --a-ink: #33223a;
    --a-ink-mid: #61506a;
    --a-ink-soft: #a294a8;
    --a-line: #e3d9e4;
    --a-fill: #ffffff;
    --a-accent: #a8577a;
    --a-img-bg: #33223a;
  }
  .pg-blog .a-posts { flex: 1; min-height: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap); }
  .pg-blog .a-post { flex: 1; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--a-gap); padding-bottom: var(--a-gap); }
  .pg-blog .a-post:not(:last-child) { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--a-line); }
  /* 4:3, not 1:1. A square at this size reads as an icon sitting next to a
     title; a landscape crop reads as the post's cover image, which is what it
     is. Height-driven so all three stay identical whatever the row height. */
  .pg-blog .a-thumb { height: 76%; aspect-ratio: 4 / 3; align-self: center; flex: none; }
  .pg-blog .a-post-copy {
    flex: 1; min-width: 0;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--a-gap-tight);
  }
  /* Titles wrap on an index. Two bars where the second is short is the
     difference between a headline and a label. */
  .pg-blog .a-post-copy > .a-h-sm + .a-h-sm { background: var(--a-ink-soft); }
  .pg-blog .a-post-meta { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); margin-top: 1px; }
  .pg-blog .a-tag { width: 11px; height: 4px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--a-accent); flex: none; }

  /* ============================================================
     08 · DETAIL — OLIVE & BRASS. Palette unchanged.
     ============================================================ */
  .pg-detail {
    --a-paper: #f3f1e8;
    --a-header: #2f3524;
    --a-header-line: #2f3524;
    --a-nav-ink: #ffffff;
    --a-nav-link: #9aa088;
    --a-ink: #2f3524;
    --a-ink-mid: #5d6449;
    --a-ink-soft: #9ba18a;
    --a-line: #dcd8c8;
    --a-fill: #ffffff;
    --a-accent: #8f7d2b;
    --a-img-bg: #5a6440;
  }
  .pg-detail .a-cover { aspect-ratio: 21 / 9; flex: none; }
  /* Headline, second line and byline are one masthead unit at tight leading.
     As five equal siblings at 5px they read as five unrelated bars. */
  .pg-detail .a-head { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); flex: none; }
  .pg-detail .a-meta { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--a-gap-tight); margin-top: 1px; }
  .pg-detail .a-tag { width: 14px; height: 6px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--a-accent); flex: none; }
  .pg-detail .a-body {
    flex: 1; min-height: 0; overflow: hidden;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap);
  }
  /* The pull quote. Heavier type than body, hung off an accent rule — the one
     place in the set a rule is set in the accent, which is exactly what a
     magazine spends one on. It is also what breaks the lower half of this card
     out of being a single flat block of body texture. */
  .pg-detail .a-pull {
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--a-gap-tight);
    padding-left: var(--a-gap); flex: none;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--a-accent);
  }
  .pg-detail .a-pull .a-h-sm { background: var(--a-ink); }

  /* ---------- Wireframe → design transition ------------------------------
     The blank wireframe is the pre-load state. When the grid scrolls into
     view, BaseLayout's IntersectionObserver adds .is-inview — the same hook
     the rest of the site animates on — and each page dissolves out of its
     wireframe into its design. The whole point of the graphic is watching a
     site get designed, so the two layers cross-fade rather than cut.

     Timing has its own two tokens rather than reusing --motion-stagger-step.
     That token is 180ms, tuned for cards rising into place, and at that pace
     the whole reveal was over in ~2.4s — the blank state flashed past before
     you could register that it WAS a blank state, which is the one thing
     this graphic exists to show. The reveal now runs on a slower step and a
     long hold, ~4.5s end to end for all eight.

     --pg-settle is a deliberate hold on the finished wireframe: the card has
     already risen and landed (that runs on --motion-stagger-step, 180ms, so
     it is well clear) and then simply sits there blank before resolving. */
  .svc-pages {
    --pg-step: 340ms;
    --pg-settle: 900ms;
  }
  .svc-pages.is-inview .pg-wire {
    animation: pgWireOut 760ms var(--ease-soft) both;
    animation-delay: calc(var(--pg-step) * var(--i) + var(--pg-settle));
  }
  .svc-pages.is-inview .pg-art {
    animation: pgArtIn 1200ms var(--ease-out-expo) both;
    animation-delay: calc(var(--pg-step) * var(--i) + var(--pg-settle));
  }
  /* Blurring the wireframe on its way out is what sells it as an unresolved
     draft rather than a layer someone turned off. */
  @keyframes pgWireOut {
    from { opacity: 1; filter: blur(0); }
    to { opacity: 0; filter: blur(1.4px); }
  }
  /* The design resolves from soft to sharp — the visual of a page rendering.
     This used to scale up from 0.98, which was fine when the layer was
     transparent; now that it paints its own paper to the card edge, a scale
     would flash a hairline of the card's white behind the corners on the way
     in. Blur has no geometry, so it cannot. */
  @keyframes pgArtIn {
    from { opacity: 0; filter: blur(1.6px); }
    to { opacity: 1; filter: blur(0); }
  }

  /* Reduced motion: skip the transition entirely and ship the designed state.
     The global reduce block in global.css collapses animation DURATION but
     not animation-delay, so without this the designs would sit invisible for
     up to 3.3s before snapping in — worse than no animation at all, and the
     longer the reveal gets the worse that failure mode is. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .pg-wire { display: none; }
    .pg-art { opacity: 1; }
    .svc-pages.is-inview .pg-wire,
    .svc-pages.is-inview .pg-art { animation: none; }
  }

  .svc-pages-cap {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--text-step--2);
    letter-spacing: 0.12em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-fg-muted);
  }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .svc-pages { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 0.4rem; }
    .pg { --pg-pad: 5px; border-radius: 6px; }
    .pg-wire { gap: 3px; }
    .pg-media { display: none; }
    /* Roughly 70px of content width per card here. The nav's fixed-width bits
       are the only thing that does not scale, so they come down; the two rows
       of trailing detail below turn to mush at this size and come out. */
    /* Both steps of the scale come down together, so every gap on every page
       shrinks in proportion and the rhythm established at desktop survives. */
    .pg-art { --a-gap: 3px; --a-gap-tight: 2px; }
    .a-nav { height: 11px; }
    .a-logo { width: 8px; height: 2px; margin-left: 6px; }
    .a-logo::before { width: 4px; height: 4px; }
    .a-links { gap: 2px; }
    .a-links > span { width: 4px; }
    .a-navcta { width: 10px; height: 6px; }
    .a-foot { height: 8px; }
    .a-foot-mark { width: 6px; }
    .a-foot-links { gap: 2px; }
    .a-foot-links > span { width: 3px; }
    /* The bands that turn to mush below ~70px of card width come out. */
    .pg-home .a-cards, .pg-home .a-strip { display: none; }
    .pg-detail .a-pull { display: none; }
    .pg-blog .a-post-meta { display: none; }
    .pg-pricing .a-plan { margin-block: 4px; padding: 4px 3px; }
    .pg-pricing .a-feats { gap: var(--a-gap-tight); }
    /* Four ruled rows in ~25px is a smudge. The columns are the point of this
       card; the FAQ is what fills the room they do not need at desktop. */
    .pg-pricing .a-faq { display: none; }
    .pg-pricing .a-plans { flex: 1; min-height: 0; }
    /* Same trade on the other two: the band that exists to absorb height goes
       first, and the image band gets it back. */
    .pg-about .a-close { display: none; }
    .pg-gallery .a-more { display: none; }
    .pg-gallery .a-mosaic .a-img:nth-child(n + 7) { display: none; }
  }

  /* ============================================================
     SECTION HEADS — the same two-column grid the homepage uses.

     This page never had it. .section-head's grid lives in index.astro's and
     work.astro's scoped styles, and scoped CSS does not reach another route;
     global.css only sets margin-bottom and a gap, and gap does nothing on a
     block element. So both heads here stacked the sub under the title at full
     measure, leaving the whitespace beside the title empty. Copied verbatim
     from index.astro so the three pages stay one rule.
     ============================================================ */
  .section-head { display: grid; gap: 1rem; grid-template-columns: 1fr; align-items: end; margin-bottom: clamp(2.5rem, 5vh, 3.75rem); }
  @media (min-width: 760px) { .section-head { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.1fr; gap: 3rem; } }

  /* ============================================================
     THE OFFER — bento spec sheet. Mirrors the homepage services
     section: a dark centerpiece (price + CTA) and six annotation
     tiles, each with its own mouse-spotlight sheen.
     ============================================================ */
  /* Top padding matches the head's own margin-bottom below, so the title sits
     the same distance from the section's top edge as it does from the cards. */
  /* Was 45/81. The short top is deliberate — this band follows the hero and
     the two read as one unit — so it keeps a shoulder above and a full step
     below, now expressed on the scale. */
  .section-offer { padding-block: var(--band-sm) var(--band-lg); }
  .section-offer .section-head { margin-bottom: clamp(2.5rem, 5vh, 4rem); }

  .offer-bento {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    grid-auto-rows: minmax(0, auto);
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .offer-bento { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 1.25rem; }
  }
  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .offer-bento {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
      grid-template-rows: repeat(3, minmax(180px, 1fr));
      gap: 1.5rem;
    }
  }

  .bento-card {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    isolation: isolate;
    --bg: #fff;
    --fg: var(--color-navy);
    --fg-soft: var(--color-fg-soft);
    --fg-muted: var(--color-fg-muted);
    --line: var(--color-line);
    --accent: var(--color-blue);
    --sheen: rgb(13 47 69 / 0.10);
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--fg);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 14px;
    transition: transform 360ms var(--ease-soft), box-shadow 360ms var(--ease-soft), border-color 360ms var(--ease-soft);
  }
  .bento-card:hover {
    transform: translateY(-3px);
    box-shadow: 0 30px 60px -28px rgb(7 23 34 / 0.28);
  }
  .bento-card .tier-sheen {
    position: absolute; inset: 0;
